# Expo UI Rules

## 1. SCOPE

These rules apply **only** to the Expo / React Native app (`apps/mobile/`).
Never apply them to the Next.js web app (`apps/web/`). When in doubt about
which app a file belongs to, skip it rather than guess.

## 2. DARK MODE

- Never write hardcoded color values (`black`, `white`, `#hex`, `rgb(…)`) in
  StyleSheet objects or inline `style` props.
- All colors must come from `useThemeColor("token")` (from `heroui-native`) or
  HeroUI semantic Tailwind classes (`text-foreground`, `bg-background`,
  `text-muted`, `bg-surface`, `bg-accent`, etc.).
- Lucide icons must receive `color={useThemeColor("…")}`. Never pass a string
  literal color to an icon.
- `TextInput` / `Input` placeholders require
  `placeholderTextColor={useThemeColor("field-placeholder")}`.
- Before marking any new component as done, mentally render it in dark mode and
  confirm every element is visible and correctly contrasted.

## 3. HEROUI NATIVE USAGE

- Import HeroUI components from `heroui-native` only. Never import from
  `@heroui/react` or any other HeroUI package.
- Always pass a `size` prop to `Button` (`size="sm" | "md" | "lg"`).
- Never override a HeroUI component's background or padding with local
  Tailwind/StyleSheet classes unless a specific one-off deviation is genuinely
  required. Any such override must be accompanied by an inline comment
  explaining the reason.
- `ThemeProvider` and `AppHeroUIProvider` live at the root (`src/app/_layout.tsx`).
  Never move, duplicate, or add a second provider lower in the tree.
- Never pass conflicting layout classes to the same element (e.g. `self-start`
  and `mx-auto` simultaneously).

## 4. COMPONENT HYGIENE

- Do not build custom primitive components (Button, Card, Badge, etc.) that
  replicate what `heroui-native` already provides. Use the library component.
- Do not add wrapper `View` elements unless they contribute measurable layout
  value: flex direction, alignment, gap, or padding that cannot be expressed
  on the parent or child directly.
- Do not copy-paste style objects across files. Extract shared styles to
  `apps/mobile/src/styles/shared.ts`.
- Do not leave dead data fields in TypeScript types or data arrays that are
  declared but never read.

## 5. UI COPY POLICY

- UI text is **functional only**: labels, data values, error messages, and
  single-word or single-phrase action cues.
- Do not add:
  - Descriptive subtitles that restate the screen title or button label
  - Helper text under inputs explaining the field purpose
  - Section intro paragraphs explaining what the section does
  - Instructional or motivational copy outside a dedicated onboarding flow
- Empty states: one short phrase maximum (e.g. "No items yet"). Nothing longer.
- Onboarding copy belongs only in dedicated onboarding screens. It must not
  appear on tab screens, settings screens, or auth screens.

## 6. FUTURE GENERATION BEHAVIOUR

The first output of any new screen or component must already comply with all
rules above. Do not generate a working draft and expect a subsequent cleanup
pass. Produce clean output from the start.
